<div style="text-align: left;">
<p>So at the end, you mentioned how the team members slowly disappears, and grow more and more suspicious of each other, till finally the captain is the lone survivor. This feels like a very cheap ending, and the overall message of your story changes radically. It's now less about trying to reconcile the differences between people of differing views, and more like hey, people are destined to never be able to see eye to eye. Which admittedly, is less of a sell for me.</p>
<p>Also, this sudden disappearance of the team members, from either the other team members, or the monsters, just&#8230; doesn't add up. You mentioned how they suspect that their own team members may have been secretly tasked to kill them, but makes the story so much more convoluted, and frankly doesn't make sense. Isn't killing them going to create more problems for both sides? No one benefits in any way. And, these are GOC soldiers. Who normally go for psych screenings, have been put in similar situations. You could say that they're better than the average human being, both in terms of physical and mental capability. I could imagine a heated arguement turned into a struggle, but murder is pretty far off.</p>
<p>And to echo what Cyvstvi said earlier, the monsters are a minor aspect of the story. In this story, it's technically the primary objective, but as it progresses, you can see that it becomes a secondary objective, compared to the resolution of the dispute between the teammates.</p>
